## Introduction
Imines and their derivatives are important for the synthesis of fine chemicals [1], such as pharmaceuticals, fragrances, fungicides, and dyes, due to their highly reactive $\mathrm{C=N}$ double bonds [2]. Photocatalytic synthesis of imine through the oxidative amine coupling is regarded as a green, sustainable, and environment friendly route in contrast to the traditional aldimine/ketoamine condensation or oxidation of amines, which often cause environmental pollution and require harsh conditions and high energyinputs.Atpresent, some visible-light-driven semiconductors including $\mathrm{MS}_{x}$ 1 $\mathbf{\Delta}\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{W}$ and Mo) [3-5], $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ [6], $\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}$ [7],conjugated polymers [8],metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) [9],and covalent organic frameworks (COFs) [10], which effectively convert optical energy into available chemical energy, have been used as photocatalysts for the selective oxidation of amines to imines under aerobic condition [3,1l]. Moreover, a variety of strategies such as heterojunctions, heteroatom doping, and nanostructuring design have been developed to effectively improve the catalytic and electronic performances of these semiconductors [12,l3]. However, it still remains a great challenge for a photocatalyst to surmount the bottlenecks in the photocatalytic oxidative coupling of benzylamine, especially the high recombination of photoexcited electrons and holes, and the kinetic shielding arising from the poor porosity of inorganic semiconductors, which greatly hinder their practical applications [14-16]. Consequently, it is highly desirable for the design and synthesis of porous photocatalysts or porous material-supported semiconductors with reactant-accessible photocatalytic sites and enhanced charge separation and transfer eficiency.
Photoactive MOFs [17]，including PCN-222 [18],NU-1000 [19],MIL-100 [20], $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 [21]，etc., are highly porous crystalline materials constructed by metal-oxo clusters and organic ligands, which can allow for electronical excitation and function as redox-typephotosensitizers.Thephoto-excited electrons produced by the light absorption of ligand can pour into metaloxo clusters to generate the charge separated state and show semiconductor behavior [22].Different from the ultraviolet light absorption of inorganic semiconductor materials, MOFs always exhibit a strong visible light absorption capacity [23]. Moreover, MOFs featuring highly diversified structures [24], large specific surface area [25,26], and adjustable meso/micropores for diffusion of substrates and products have demonstrated their potential as photocatalyst for oxidative amine coupling [27]. Unfortunately, MOFs suffer from low charge separation efficiency and high recombination of photogenerated electrons and holes [28-30]. Even though some successful photocatalytic MOF-based heterojunctions have been constructed with various strategies, such as nanoparticle (NP)/MOF [31], NP@MOF [32], and $\mathrm{NP}@\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{NP}$ [16]，the yolk-shellheterojunction reduction@MOF@oxidation $\operatorname{'RE@MOF@OX})$ with spatially separated cocatalysts via integration of reduction and oxidation cocatalysts inside and outside of MOFs, respectively, is believed to be capable of enhancing the efficiency of photo energy utilization theoretically and experimentally [15]. In the composite, oxidative cocatalysts act as hole collectors, and reductive cocatalysts conduct as electron trappers. The accumulating of photoexcited electrons and holes in the opposite direction favors the separation of photogenerated electrons and holes [l6,33]. Notably, Bu group reported a heterostructured photocatalyst $\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO$66@\mathrm{MnO}_{x}$ with spatially separated cocatalysts $\mathrm{Pt}$ and $\mathrm{MnO}_{x},$ facilitating the overall water splitting [22]. We envisioned that by taking advantage of the high porosity of MOFs, the design of yolk-shell MOF-based photocatalyst would be an effective way to improve the charge separation efficiency and retain the highly porosity for the photocatalytic oxidative coupling of benzylamine.
Herein, we design and synthesize yolk-shell $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO$66@\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ composites, in which Pd NPs are trapped inside $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ UiO-66 as electron collectors, and $\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ NPs are located on the surface of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 as hole collectors. $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO$66@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ can effectively separate photogenerated electrons and holes with high charge separation efficiency, especially, catalyze the oxidation of benzylamine under both aerobic and anaerobic conditions. This work provides experimental data and theoretical support for realizing the spatial separation of electrons and holes during photocatalytic organic conversion reactions.
## 2 Experimental methods
## 2.1 Materials and physical measurements
Some chemicals and reagents, such as zirconium tetrachloride $\mathrm{(ZrCl_{4})}$ , 2-aminoterephthalic acid ( $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC), palladium chloride $\mathrm{(PdCl_{2})}$ ,N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF), and absolute ethanol $(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{OH})$ ，were used as received without further purification. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images were obtained on a field-emission scanning electron microanalyzer. The synthesized samples were uniformly dispersed in ethanol solution $\mathrm{(1~mg/mL)}$ by ultrasound for $30\mathrm{min}.$ and then dropped onto the ultrathin Cu net. Then, the morphology and microstructure of the samples were characterized by transmission electron microscopy (TEM) (JEM-2100, Japan). The $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ desorption isotherms of the samples were measured by the BELSORP-max adsorption instrument and the pore size distribution and specific surface area of the samples were analyzed by the software of the instrument itself. The temperature of the adsorption samples was controlled at $77\mathrm{K}$ by liquid nitrogen. The ratio of substrate to product in the catalytic reaction was analyzed using $400\mathrm{MHz}$ Bruker Avance-400 nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR） spectroscopy, and the chemical shift of which was calibrated with tetramethylsilane as internal standard 1 $\mathrm{{(0~ppm)}}$ . The crystallinity and purity of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 and its composites were studied by means of Dutch PANalytical powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) with wavelength of 1.5418 A Cu Kα. The X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was tested on the Axis Supra instrument and the full spectrum and fine spectrum for each element were calibrated with vacuum carbon as internal standard $(284.6~\mathrm{~eV})$ . The ultraviolet-visible (UV-vis） diffuse reflectivity profile was obtained using the JASCO V-750 UV-vis spectrophotometer. The contents of Pd NPs and $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ NPs in $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO- $66@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ composites  were  measured  using Optimass 9500 inductively coupled plasma-optical emission spectroscopy (ICP-OES). Electrochemical characterizations such as impedance, transient photocurrent, and Mott-Schottky (M-S) curves were tested on the Chenhua CHI 600e electrochemical workstation. The electrolyte tested was $0.1\ \mathrm{mol/L}$ sodium sulfate aqueous solution. The electron spin resonance spectra of the material were measured on the Bruker A300 spin resonance spectrometer and the superoxide free radical, singlet oxygen, and hydroxyl radicals  were  measured by the addition of dimethylpyridine nitrogen oxides.
