## 3 Results and discussion
3.1 Preparation and characterization of $\mathbf{Pd@NH_{2}}$ -UiO${\bf66@C u_{2}O}$ and $\mathbf{Pd}/\mathbf{NH_{2}}\mathbf{-UiO-}66@\mathbf{Cu}_{2}\mathbf{O}$
For the synthesis of photocatalysts with reduction-oxidation cocatalyst spatial separation, a protocol using porous photoactive $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 to separate $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and Pd NPs was applied. Composed of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\upmu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\upmu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}$ $\left(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\right)$ as a metaloxygen cluster and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC as a connector, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 exhibits excellent chemical stability over a wide $\mathrm{\pH}$ range and has a high surface area of about $800~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ [36]. In addition, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO66 can effectively absorb light in the visible region [37]. Herein, a ternary yolk-shell composites $P\mathrm{d}@\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{UiO}{-}66@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was synthesized, in which Pd NPs were embedded inside $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 as electron collectors, and $\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ NPs were located on the surface of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 as hole collectors (Scheme 1).
In a typical experiment, a one-pot synthesis of $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}},$ $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ BDC, and DMF solution containing presynthesized Pd NPs yielded “ship in a bottle” $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66,which was deposited by $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ NPs on the surface by means of photodeposition, providing $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{UiO}{-}66@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as dark-brown powders. For comparison,“bottlearound ship” $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66was synthesized by reducing $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PdCl}_{4}$ with ethanol to deposit Pd NPs on the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 surface. Subsequently, a layer of $\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ NPs was covered on $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 by the same photodeposition method to obtain $\mathrm{Pd/NH_{2}\mathrm{-UiO-}66@C u_{2}O}$ . The contents of Pd NPs and $\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ NPs in the $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO- $66@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ composite were about $0.3\mathrm{wt}.\%$ and $0.8\mathrm{wt}.\%$ , respectively, confirmed by ICPOES.
The PXRD patterns of ${\mathrm{Pd}}\ @{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ -UiO- $66@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ 。 $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-
$66@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{:}$ $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66, and $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 were in good agreement with the UiO-66 simulation and all samples showed high crystalinity, which indicated that the frame structure of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ UiO-66 was well maintained after integration (Fig. 1(a)) [38]. The characteristic peaks of Pd and $\mathrm{{Cu_{2}O}}$ NPs were not observed in the PXRD patterns because of their high dispersion and low concentration [39]. SEM image shows that $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO$66@\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ is an octahedron composed of nanoscale tiny particles with a size of about $20\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig.1(b)). High-resolution TEM (HRTEM) images show the clear lattice spacings 0.224 and $0.21\ \mathrm{nm},$ corresponding to (1ll) lattice fringes of Pd and $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}O}$ NPs, respectively (Figs. 1(c) and 1(d)), which prove the existence of $\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ and Pd NPs [32,40]. Energy dispersion X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) confirms that elements N, O, Zr, Pd, and Cu are uniformly distributed in $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-UiO-}66@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ composites (Figs. 1(e)-1(i)).
