## 3.2 Photocatalytic oxidative coupling of amines
To evaluate the catalytic activity of $P\mathrm{d}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-UiO-}66@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ for the oxidative coupling of amines to imines, benzylamine was chosen as a substrate. When $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66， $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66, $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66，and $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO- $66@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ were used as catalysts, the yields of imines were $40\%$ 。 $26\%.$ $42\%$ 。and $33\%$ respectively. As expected, with $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-UiO-}66@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as a catalyst, the yield of imine could be as high as $99\%$ .This was ascribed to the fact that the spatial separation $\mathrm{Pd}$ and $\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ cocatalysts could reduce the recombination of $^{\mathrm{e-h}}$ pairs and improve efficiency of charge separation. Blank experiments in the absence of either light or catalyst showed only slight conversion of the substrates, confirming that the conversion was driven by the photocatalytic process.
We varied the amount of deposited $\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ by changing the illumination time into 3 and $^\mathrm{~7~h~}$ on $\mathrm{CuSO_{4}}$ solution. $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ UiO- ${.66@\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}}{-3\mathrm{h}}$ and $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{NH_{2}}\mathrm{-UiO-66@Cu_{2}O-7h}$ were obtained and the contents of $\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ NPs were 0.7 wt $\%$ and $0.9\ \mathrm{wt}.\%$ ， respectively, as determined by ICP-OES. Subsequently, the catalytic activity of $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO- $66@\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}{-3}\mathrm{h}$ and $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ UiO- $66@\mathrm{Cu_{2}O-7h}$ on the photocatalytic oxidative coupling of benzylamine was evaluated. The results show that the yields of $34\%$ and $28\%$ are obtained for ${\mathrm{Pd}}\ @{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ -UiO- $66@\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}-3\mathrm{h}$ and ${\mathrm{Pd}}\ @{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ -UiO- $66@\mathrm{Cu_{2}O-7h}.$ 、 respectively. The lower catalytic activity of $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-UiO-}66@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O-}3\mathrm{h}$ could be ascribed to the reduced amount of $\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ NPs, which provide inadequate active sites in the catalyst. Otherwise, the excess $\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ NPs of $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ UiO- $66@\mathrm{Cu_{2}O-7h}$ blocked the micropores of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 and reduced the catalytic yield.
We further studied the catalytic activity of $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO$66@\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ with substituted benzylamine under the same reaction conditions. As shown in Table 2, $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{NH_{2}}\mathrm{-UiO-66@Cu_{2}O}$ exhibited excellent catalytic performance for benzylamines with o-, m-, and p-methyl and electron-withdrawing groups, such as $-\mathrm{CF}_{3},$ -F,and $^{-\mathrm{Cl}}$ (entries l-7 in Table 2). However, electrondonating group -OMe at the para position of benzylamine could hardly be converted into the target product (entry 9 in Table 2). In addition, this performance also indicated that when $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ UiO- $66@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was used as catalyst, steric hindrance effect was also the key factor to determine its catalytic efficiency. Compared with monosubstituted chlorine, 3,4-dichlorobenzylamine was converted into the target product in a low yield of $27\%$ (entry 8 in Table 2).
Next,we used ESR capture technology to detect the main free radicals of the photocatalytic reaction with DMPO or TEMP as a trapping agent (Fig.4). As shown in Figs. 4(a)-4(c), reactive oxygen species (ROS), such as $\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\cdot-}$ ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ and ·OH, were not present with $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-UiO-}66@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as the sample in the dark condition [45].However, these radicals could be detected after irradiation, indicating that the reaction was a photocatalytic process. When benzylamine was added into the light system, the intensity of free radicals decreased to a certain extent, indicating that these three free radicals were active species in the catalytic system. For comparation, we used $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66， $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66，and $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-UiO-}66@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as the samples for ESR testing, respectively (Figs. 4(d)-4(f)). It could be seen that $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO$66@\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ showed an obvious increasing of intensity signals of $\mathrm{DMPO-O_{2}}^{-}$ TEMP- $\cdot^{1}\mathrm{{O}}_{2},$ and DMPO--OH than those of $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ UiO-66 and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66. The results indicated that the special separation of Pd and $\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ NPs by $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 could promote the generation of ROS. In contrast to $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ UiO-66, $P\mathrm{d}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-UiO-}66@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ generated more free radicals per unit time and thus accelerated the oxidation reaction of benzylamine. In order to obtain more information about the photocatalytic process, radical scavengers including pbenzoquinone [46], 2-methylfuran $\mathrm{(C_{5}H_{6}O)}$ ，tert-butanol $((\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{3}\mathrm{OH})$ ，silver nitrate $(\mathrm{AgNO}_{3})$ ,and potassium iodide $(\mathrm{KI})$ were introduced (Table 1). Firstly, we added ${\mathrm{C}}_{5}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}{\mathrm{O}}$ or $(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ into the catalytic reaction, and the decrease of imine yields to $29\%$ and $33\%$ 。respectively,was due to the reduction of $\mathrm{ROS}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and .OH. In addition, $\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}$ was added to the reaction to prevent electron transfer to $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ As expected, we observed a drop in yield to $28\%$ 。indicating that the reaction occurs by excited state electron transfer. Further application of KI as a hole scavenger showed that the yield of the reaction decreased to $<10\%$ which might be due to the reduction of photogenerated holes and the failure of benzylamine to be further oxidized to generate benzylamine cation radicals [17].
To our surprise, after adding p-benzoquinone to the photocatalytic system, the yield of imine could still reach $99\%$ .It could be speculated that the reaction might follow an alternative reaction route. For the purpose of lucubrating the catalytic activity of $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO- $66@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ on the reaction in the absence of oxygen, the catalytic reaction was carried out in the Ar under irradiation of xenon lamp for $1,4,6,8,10,$ and $^{12\mathrm{h},}$ respectively. The gas chromatograph showed that no hydrogen production was detected during the catalytic reaction. NMR results showed that a yield of $99\%$ could be achieved in an hour. Furthermore, secondary amine was obtained at 4 and $6\mathrm{{h}}$ as the by-products in the yield of less than $5\%$ and $14\%$ respectively. When expanding the irradiation time to more than $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ imine was obtained as the solo product (Fig.5). The formation of secondary amine clearly indicated that $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ on surface of Pd NPs was produced by the deprotonation of benzylamine. Notably, Su group reported a catalyst $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4},$ which exhibited strong affinity for both imines and $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{ads}},$ leading to the formation of secondary amines [47]. Furthermore, when we added $(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{3}\mathrm{COH}$ to the catalytic reaction under anaerobic conditions, the yield of imine could still reach $99\%$ . The result excluded the formation of -OH radicals in anaerobic conditions for the oxidative coupling of amines.
Based on the above-mentioned results, a possible mechanism for coupling of benzylamine to imines over the $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO$66@\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ under both aerobic and anaerobic conditions is proposed (Scheme 2). Under aerobic conditions, when $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO66 is photoexcited, the excited electrons are collected by the electron collectors Pd NPs and then transfer to $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ producing $\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}$ 。 ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ and -OH.Meanwhile, their holes are collected by the hole collectors $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ NPs,which catalyze the conversion of benzylaminetocarbocationic-radical-type intermediate. Subsequently, $\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}$ ， $^{1}{\bf O}_{2},$ and -OH oxidize the intermediate to generate $\mathrm{PHCH=NH}$ intermediate and by-product $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (Fig.S6 in the ESM) [48]. In order to study the effect of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ on the amine oxidation reaction, we carried out the comparative experiments by adding $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ 1 $\left(0.025\ \mathrm{\mmol}\right)$ into the amine oxidation reaction system. The experimental results show that under the same conditions, the yield of imine with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is only $36\%$ . It indicates that the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ can inhibit the amine oxidation reaction. $\mathrm{PHCH=NH}$ may generate imine in two ways. One route is to remove $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ through the coupling of $\mathrm{PHCH=NH}$ with another benzylamine [5].
Alternatively, the other route is to obtain aldehydes by $\mathrm{PHCH=NH}$ hydrolysis and obtain the imine by condensation with another molecule of benzylamine. On the other hand, under anaerobic and irradiation conditions, benzylamine is first oxidized into $\mathrm{PHCH=NH}$ on $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO- $66@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ through initial deprotonation, and the released protons are transferred to the surface of the composites, and finally reduced to $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ by lightgenerated electrons. $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ molecules on $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-UiO-}66@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ can be retained due to their strong adsorption capacity [15]. Subsequently, nucleophilic attack on $\mathrm{PHCH=NH}$ by another benzylamine leads to the formation of imines, accompanied by the release of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ ：Finally， $P\mathrm{d}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-UiO-}66@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ further hydrogenates the retained $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ into secondary amines. After prolonging, secondary amines release $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ and are reoxidized to
imines.
Finally, the reusability of $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO- $66@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was evaluated by cycling the photocatalytic reaction for ten times under the same reaction conditions. As seen from Fig. S7(a) in the ESM, the yield of the product remained above $99\%$ even after ten cycles. Moreover, the PXRD patterns, Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectrum, and XPS spectrum (Figs. S7(b)-S7(i) in the ESM）of $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-UiO-}66@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ remained unchanged [49], suggesting that the catalyst was well preserved and had good stability to undergo a long-term reaction.
## 4 Conclusions
In conclusion, a highly porous and crystalline yolk-shell $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$
UiO- $66@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ composite is designed and constructed to achieve the spatial separation of cocatalysts, i.e., the inner and outer surfaces of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 shell are embedded with Pd NPs and coated with $\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ NPs, respectively, to promote the generation and separation of photoinduced $^{\mathrm{e-h}}$ pairs. In addition, the highly porous structure of $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{UiO}{-}66@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ can provide stable channels for the diffusion of amine and imine, and large specific surface area to provide more attachment sites for Pd NPs and $\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ NPs． $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-UiO-}66@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ shows  sgnificantly enhanced photocatalytic oxidation of benzylamine to imine under both aerobic and anaerobic condition. Under aerobic reaction condition, the ROS, mainly consisting of ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2},\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-};$ and ·OH, are generated by energy or charge transfer processes on the conduction band (CB) of photocatalysts. These ROS then oxidize amine and couple to form imines. Under anaerobic condition, the hole collectors Pd can active amine molecules by oxidative deprotonation. This work not only demonstrates a simple method to prepare photocatalysts with spatially separated cocatalysts, but also provides an efficient protocol for photocatalytic synthesis of imines with or without oxygen.
